#!/usr/bin/env python3
# =====================================================================================
# BREACHPOINT - render_weapons.py :: render the weapon silhouettes from the meshes
#
# Serves:   docs/ui/WEAPON-RENDER-PLAN.md (Tier 4 of ASSET-METHODS.md). Runs INSIDE Unreal
#           (it imports `unreal`).
# Law:      CLAUDE.md law 3 (soft refs), law 5 (owner paths - /Game/UI is BP10's),
#           law 7 (assets are generated by committed scripts, never hand-placed).
# Rulings:  R37 (a committed plan specifies the asset; a receipt is committed with it),
#           R21/R29/R36 (an open editor and anything that takes the project lock must not
#           overlap - and this commandlet TAKES THE LOCK without being a build).
#
# ############################### THIS IS A SPIKE #####################################
# #                                                                                   #
# #  NOTHING IN THIS FILE HAS EVER BEEN RUN. No editor was opened to write it. Every  #
# #  `unreal` API name below was taken from the 5.8 docs and IS UNVERIFIED. The first #
# #  run is expected to FAIL and to correct section 3.3 of the plan document - that   #
# #  is the intended shape, not a defect (WEAPON-RENDER-PLAN.md section 9.5).         #
# #                                                                                   #
# #  Consequently it is written to FAIL LOUDLY AND EARLY rather than half-produce an  #
# #  icon: every binding it cannot resolve is a named refusal, every assumption is    #
# #  numbered below and cited at the line that depends on it, and the receipt records #
# #  the refusals as carefully as the successes. A receipt showing only the calls     #
# #  that worked is an advertisement, not a record (section 6.2).                     #
# #####################################################################################
#
# THE ASSUMPTIONS. Each is a thing the first run will confirm or destroy. They are cited
# as "A<n>" at the code that rests on them.
#
#   A1  A `-run=pythonscript` commandlet has an editor world we may spawn actors into.
#       If it does not, everything below the mesh load is unreachable and the fix is a
#       different capture strategy (an offscreen world, or UMG/thumbnail rendering).
#   A2  `unreal.SceneCaptureComponent2D` with ORTHO projection + ShowOnlyActors renders a
#       single actor cleanly, and `capture_scene()` is synchronous enough that the render
#       target holds the frame on the next line.
#   A3  `/Game/LevelPrototyping/Materials/M_FlatCol` is an UNLIT constant-colour material
#       whose colour can be forced white. NOBODY HAS READ ITS PARAMETERS. If it is lit, or
#       has no colour parameter, the render is not a flat mask and section 3.3 is wrong.
#   A4  ShowFlags on a capture component are settable from Python by the names used below
#       (`lighting`, `temporal_aa`, `anti_aliasing`, `post_processing`). Several of these
#       are properties on `ShowFlags`-like structs in C++ and may not be exposed at all.
#   A5  `flip_x` can be done with a negative actor scale. Winding/normals invert, which is
#       harmless for a flat unlit mask and would NOT be harmless for a lit render.
#   A6  A PNG decoder is importable inside the editor's Python (PIL, else numpy + the
#       engine's own readback). If neither exists, the supersampled render is preserved
#       on disk and the run fails with POSTPROCESS_UNAVAILABLE - it does not pretend.
#   A7  The mesh bounds returned by `get_bounds()` are LOCAL and in unreal units, and the
#       pivot may be anywhere (muzzle, grip, origin). The framing math only uses extents,
#       so a strange pivot shifts the weapon off-centre WITHOUT changing its size - which
#       is exactly what the first render will show if A7's second half is wrong.
#
# Headless (the crew's default):
#   UnrealEditor-Cmd.exe <repo>\Breachpoint.uproject ^
#       -run=pythonscript -script="<repo>\Tools\render_weapons\render_weapons.py" ^
#       -stdout -unattended -nosplash -nop4 -NoLiveCoding
# The wrapper Tools/render_weapons/render-weapons.ps1 does exactly this, plus engine
# resolution, the plugin check, the R21 editor guard and logging. USE THE WRAPPER: the raw
# form bypasses the guard.
#
# Exit codes: 0 rendered · 1 refused / failed / contract violation · 3 blocked (bad env).
# =====================================================================================
